    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d718">

                <pb facs="0679" n="671" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg671"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/1698: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-10-17T20:00:00+03:00">October 17, 1940—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1361. In the course of a conversation with the Chinese Ambassador<anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d718-f1"/> this afternoon he stated that he had again seen
                        Vishinsky<anchor n="88" xml:id="d718-f2"/> who in reassuring him as to the
                    continuance of Soviet aid to China had remarked that “while the Soviet methods
                    might have to undergo a change the Soviet goal remained the same.”</p>
                <p>The Ambassador stated in so far as he was aware there had been no recent
                    appreciable diminution in Soviet aid to China but that on the other hand the
                    Soviet Government had not responded to his request for better types of airplanes
                    (see my 1235, September 25, 11 p.m.<anchor n="89" xml:id="d718-f3"/>).</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d718-f1" n="87" xml:id="d718-f1-content">
                <p>Shao Li-tzu.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d718-f2" n="88" xml:id="d718-f2-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Soviet Vice Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d718-f3" n="89" xml:id="d718-f3-content">
                <p>Printed in vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, section under Union of
                    Soviet Socialist Republics entitled “Reports on Developments of Significance
                    Concerning Soviet Relations With Other Countries, Especially With the United
                    States.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
